From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 01:20:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CUDA: keep CUDA graphs for MUL_MAT_ID paths that never
 synchronise the stream

ggml_cuda_graph_check_compability() rejects CUDA graphs for the WHOLE graph as
soon as it sees one MUL_MAT_ID node whose src0 is not quantized:

    const int mmvq_mmid_max = get_mmvq_mmid_max_batch(node->src[0]->type, cc);
    if (!ggml_is_quantized(node->src[0]->type) || node->ne[2] > mmvq_mmid_max) {

The stated reason is that "the mul_mat_id operation will need to synchronize the
stream". That is true of exactly one code path: the sorted fallback at the tail
of ggml_cuda_mul_mat_id(), which does cudaMemcpyAsync of the id tensor into a
std::vector followed by cudaStreamSynchronize. It is NOT true of the four fast
paths that return before it - ggml_cuda_mul_mat_vec_q, ggml_cuda_mul_mat_vec_f,
ggml_cuda_mul_mat_q and ggml_cuda_mul_mat_f. mmq.cu, mmf.cu, mmvq.cu and mmvf.cu
contain no cudaStreamSynchronize and no device-to-host copy at all; the id
handling they need is done on the device by mm_ids_helper. The predicate is
simply coarser than the dispatch it is trying to describe: it accepts only the
quantized mul_mat_vec_q path and rejects the other three.

This replaces it with ggml_cuda_mul_mat_id_is_stream_sync_free(), which mirrors
the early-return conditions of ggml_cuda_mul_mat_id() term for term, so graphs
are rejected if and only if the op would really reach the synchronising
fallback. GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_MMID_GRAPHS=1 restores the stock acceptance in the
same binary, which is how the arms below were separated.

Why this model hits it
----------------------
Laguna S 2.1 is not uniformly quantized. Its GGUF tensor census (read from the
tensor directory of laguna-s-2.1-Q4_K_M.gguf, sha256 a8b55c75...83e4) is:

     39x  blk.N.ffn_{gate,up,down}_exps   (3072,1024,256)   Q4_K   16.45 GiB
      8x  blk.N.ffn_{gate,up,down}_exps   (3072,1024,256)   BF16   12.00 GiB

Eight of the forty-eight blocks carry BF16 experts. ggml_is_quantized(BF16) is
false, so those twenty-four MUL_MAT_ID nodes disable CUDA graphs for the entire
decode graph - every one of the ~1,400 kernel launches per decoded token is
issued individually. The sibling track laguna-xs-2.1 has Q4_K experts in every
block and therefore keeps its graphs, which is why this only shows up here.

On NVIDIA the BF16 MUL_MAT_ID at ne2 == 1 is served by ggml_cuda_mul_mat_f
(nsys confirms: mul_mat_f<__nv_bfloat162, 32, 1, 8, true>, 24 launches per
decoded token, 13.8% of decode kernel time). That path never touches the host,
so the graph rejection was buying nothing.

Correctness
-----------
Bit-identical by construction: not one kernel, launch parameter, argument or
node ordering changes. The only thing that changes is whether the identical
sequence of launches is replayed from a captured CUDA graph.

Measured, three arms over the fixed corpus (~/gainz-ppl-corpus.txt, 29,592
bytes, 15 chunks, n_ctx 512, -ngl 99), llama-perplexity:

    stock b10237                                PPL = 5.7341 +/- 0.25780
    this patch, graphs ENABLED                  PPL = 5.7341 +/- 0.25780
    this patch, GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_MMID_GRAPHS=1 PPL = 5.7341 +/- 0.25780

Identical to every printed digit; relative delta 0.000% against a 0.5% gate.
The third arm is the same binary with the escape hatch on, which isolates the
graph enablement itself as the only variable between arms one and two.

Measurement (DGX Spark GB10, sm_121, CUDA 13.0, -ngl 99, solo window)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
llama-bench -p 512 -n 128 -r 5, interleaved, one fresh model load per arm:

    stock      tg128 24.02 +/- 0.01     pp512 618.84 +/- 4.24
    this patch tg128 24.07 +/- 0.03     pp512 617.05 +/- 3.42
    this patch tg128 25.76 +/- 0.07     pp512 632.58 +/- 13.87   (cold-box load)
    stock      tg128 23.72 +/- 0.04     pp512 587.47 +/- 65.12   (earlier window)

Stated plainly: the two arms are within noise of each other in the paired
hot-box rounds (+0.2% decode). The 25.76 candidate round was the first load
after the box had been idled and is treated here as load-time variance, not as
the patch's effect - a two-samples-per-arm local harness cannot resolve a few
percent against that spread. The trusted runner's 9-run cache-cold paired
protocol can, and that is what this submission asks it to do. The change is
submitted on its correctness argument and its mechanism; the speed claim is
explicitly NOT that it is +8%.
---
diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu
index 561ab7a..b2f7fd1 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu
@@ -2507,6 +2507,55 @@ static bool ggml_cuda_is_view_or_noop(const ggml_tensor * t) {
 }
 
 #ifdef USE_CUDA_GRAPH
+// Mirrors the early-return dispatch of ggml_cuda_mul_mat_id() [TAG_MUL_MAT_ID_CUDA_GRAPHS].
+// Returns true when the op is served by one of the four device-only fast paths
+// (mul_mat_vec_q / mul_mat_vec_f / mul_mat_q / mul_mat_f). None of those copies the id
+// tensor to the host or synchronizes the stream; only the sorted fallback at the tail of
+// ggml_cuda_mul_mat_id() does, and that is the one CUDA graphs have to reject.
+// Keep this in lockstep with ggml_cuda_mul_mat_id().
+static bool ggml_cuda_mul_mat_id_is_stream_sync_free(const ggml_tensor * dst, const int cc) {
+    static const bool disabled = getenv("GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_MMID_GRAPHS") != nullptr;
+
+    const ggml_tensor * src0 = dst->src[0];
+    const ggml_tensor * src1 = dst->src[1];
+
+    if (src1->type != GGML_TYPE_F32 || dst->type != GGML_TYPE_F32) {
+        return false;
+    }
+
+    const int64_t ne2  = dst->ne[2];
+    const int64_t ne02 = src0->ne[2];
+    const int64_t ne12 = src1->ne[2];
+
+    static_assert(MMVQ_MAX_BATCH_SIZE == MMVF_MAX_BATCH_SIZE);
+    if (ne2 <= MMVQ_MAX_BATCH_SIZE) {
+        if (ggml_is_quantized(src0->type)) {
+            if (ne2 <= get_mmvq_mmid_max_batch(src0->type, cc)) {
+                return true; // ggml_cuda_mul_mat_vec_q
+            }
+        } else {
+            if (GGML_CUDA_CC_IS_AMD(cc)) {
+                return true; // ggml_cuda_mul_mat_vec_f
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    if (disabled) {
+        // Escape hatch: restore the stock quantized-only acceptance.
+        return false;
+    }
+
+    if (ggml_cuda_should_use_mmq(src0->type, cc, ne12, /*n_experts=*/ne02)) {
+        return true; // ggml_cuda_mul_mat_q
+    }
+
+    if (ggml_cuda_should_use_mmf(src0->type, cc, WARP_SIZE, src0->ne, src0->nb, src1->ne[2], /*mul_mat_id=*/true)) {
+        return true; // ggml_cuda_mul_mat_f
+    }
+
+    return false;
+}
+
 static bool ggml_cuda_graph_check_compability(ggml_cgraph * cgraph) {
 
     bool use_cuda_graph = true;
@@ -2522,10 +2571,8 @@ static bool ggml_cuda_graph_check_compability(ggml_cgraph * cgraph) {
         // [TAG_MUL_MAT_ID_CUDA_GRAPHS]
         if (node->op == GGML_OP_MUL_MAT_ID) {
             const int cc = ggml_cuda_info().devices[ggml_cuda_get_device()].cc;
-            const int mmvq_mmid_max = get_mmvq_mmid_max_batch(node->src[0]->type, cc);
-            if (!ggml_is_quantized(node->src[0]->type) || node->ne[2] > mmvq_mmid_max) {
+            if (!ggml_cuda_mul_mat_id_is_stream_sync_free(node, cc)) {
                 // under these conditions, the mul_mat_id operation will need to synchronize the stream, so we cannot use CUDA graphs
-                // TODO: figure out a way to enable for larger batch sizes, without hurting performance
                 // ref: https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/pull/18958
                 use_cuda_graph = false;
 #ifndef NDEBUG
